On Thu, Sep 18, 2014 at 11:14 AM, Jacob Errington <nictytan@gmail.com>
wrote:

> I can see bilni in two ways.
>
> 1) bende with the x2 removed, plus the usual connotations of things being
> military. (That being said, I would like to see bende2 removed, since it's
> just another instance of group membership which can be achieved by other
> means.)
>

I have a different view on "bende", I think the x2 is important, and the x3
is the one that doesn't belong. For me bende is just a special case of
gunma, just like bakfu, stura, liste, cecmu and so many others. There are
many gismu wih an emergent-constituents relation between x1 and x2 and I
think it's a good regularity. I consider flock, herd, swarm, etc examples
of "bende", and I don't think they must necessarily have a leader. If we
need to talk about lo jatna be lo bende, we can say "lo jatna be lo bende",
or "lo bedja'a". I don't think "bende" should be burdened with a place for
a leader, because it restricts its meaning too much.

2) the definition is a bit off. Read it instead like this: "bilni = x1 is
> *a* military/regimented/strongly organized x2 for purpose x3". This way,
> bilni is what I call a "connotation selbri", like the cultural gismu. This
> allows you to use it as a compositional adjective, like mutce et al. e.g.
> bende bilni -> bilni lo ka ce'u bende -> "army". You can effectively
> "militarize" any selbri by sticking "bilni" onto the end thus.
>

Agreed, if I had been thrown the keyword "military" to make a place
structure for it I would have said "x1 is military in property/aspect x2",
because that's the usual place structure for adjectives.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

